compile-time, template-based tools used to query or modify the properties of types

1. Type Predicates (Querying Value Properties)
Unary Predicates (checks one type): std::is_integral<T>, std::is_pointer<T>, std::is_const<T>.
Binary Predicates (compares two types): std::is_same<T, U>, std::is_convertible<T, U>

2. Property Queries 
std::rank<T>: Returns the number of dimensions of an array type.
std::extent<T>: Returns the size of a specific dimension of an array type.

3. Type Transformations (Modifying Types)
std::remove_const<T> → std::remove_const_t<T>
std::add_pointer<T> → std::add_pointer_t<T>
std::decay<T> → std::decay_t<T>  removes both references and top-level const/volatile qualifiers from a type, while also converting arrays and functions into pointers

Questions:
1. "How does std::enable_if work under the hood, and what C++ mechanism does it rely on?"
It relies on SFINAE (Substitution Failure Is Not An Error). If a type trait evaluates to false, the structure template fails to substitute, 
which cleanly removes that function overload from the compiler's candidate list instead of throwing a compilation error.

2. "Implement a custom type trait like std::is_pointer or std::is_same."

#include <iostream>

// --- Custom is_same ---
template <typename T, typename U>
struct is_same {
    static constexpr bool value = false;
};

template <typename T>
struct is_same<T, T> {
    static constexpr bool value = true;
};

// --- Custom is_pointer ---
template <typename T>
struct is_pointer {
    static constexpr bool value = false;
};

template <typename T>
struct is_pointer<T*> {
    static constexpr bool value = true;
};

// CV-qualifiers variants
template <typename T> struct is_pointer<T* const> { static constexpr bool value = true; };
template <typename T> struct is_pointer<T* volatile> { static constexpr bool value = true; };
template <typename T> struct is_pointer<T* const volatile> { static constexpr bool value = true; };

// Helpers
template <typename T, typename U> inline constexpr bool is_same_v = is_same<T, U>::value;
template <typename T> inline constexpr bool is_pointer_v = is_pointer<T>::value;

int main() {
    std::cout << std::boolalpha;
    std::cout << is_same_v<int, float> << "\n";  // false
    std::cout << is_pointer_v<int*> << "\n";     // true
}

3. "What is std::decay, and why is it critical when writing a generic wrapper like a thread pool or messaging queue?"
std::decay applies the same type conversions that happen when arguments are passed by value to a function. It strips away const, volatile, and references, and converts arrays and functions into pointers. 
In trading systems, it is heavily used to store arguments in a decoupled, asynchronous queue (like a lock-free ring buffer) where you cannot risk capturing a reference to a temporary or local stack object

4. "How would you write a compile-time trait to detect if a class has a specific member function or type alias?"
template<typename T>
concept HasSerialize = requires(T a) {
    { a.serialize() } -> std::same_as<void>;
};


